				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modulum" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3072585); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3072585&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795048" alt="Modulum" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3072585)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modulum" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3072585); return false;" >Modulum</a></span> <span class="odate time_1579593854 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Jan 2020 08:04</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4475027">
									

<p>This is, in a few words, a spectacular achievement. My mind is blown, my wig has been snatched.</p>
<p>The amount of careful detail put into this story is so, so well done, and it excellently contributes to that terrible-unnatural-disturbance-in-a-sleepy-town trope of horror that you nail. This plays out like a damn movie, setting the atmosphere and the subsequent pacing of the investigations in a tight and gripping way.</p>
<p>And this is not to mention the media, which is stunning. Original images, animations, fucking <em>videos.</em> Some really inspired ideas like spectrograms and scanned documents and hotel notes. And what I like is that it feels intimately tied to the reading experience; the beginning image with the &quot;LOVE THE TOWER PRAISE THE TOWER&quot; stuff - very trope-y, but absolutely sold the eldritch creepy angle in a refreshing snappy way.</p>
<p>This hit uncanny valley feelings <em>all over.</em> The bizarre newscasts, complete with wrong-looking chilling logos - perfectly building and illustrating the feeling of takeover, the themes of propaganda and worship. The presentation - distributing details so that the situation felt gritty and real.</p>
<p>I consider this a masterful tribute to the horror genre - a simple story about a godlike entity playing with a small group/town (very reminiscent of Doctor Who, of course), but told so cinemetically, with such precision and scope, that it embodies the story completely, and is brought to life.</p>
<p>You've come so far as a writer since you started, Jackal - I'm just surprised it happened <em>this fucking quickly.</em></p>

							</div>

								
